988
00:56:55,502 --> 00:56:56,042
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a copy.

989
00:56:56,062 --> 00:56:56,903
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the copy.

990
00:56:56,923 --> 00:56:57,983
[SPEAKER_03]: From the original, you see.

991
00:56:58,003 --> 00:57:00,204
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what they can do with these originals.

992
00:57:02,124 --> 00:57:03,185
[SPEAKER_03]: So I was going to have him sign them.

993
00:57:04,673 --> 00:57:05,973
[SPEAKER_03]: I can't see him to sign them.

994
00:57:06,813 --> 00:57:08,074
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I don't know.

995
00:57:08,274 --> 00:57:10,334
[SPEAKER_01]: You're a little late for Heinegrohe, aren't you?

996
00:57:11,014 --> 00:57:13,815
[SPEAKER_01]: I saw him over at Nurtingholm.

997
00:57:14,395 --> 00:57:22,936
[SPEAKER_01]: My brother was over there at the same time he was over there, and he died over there, and my brother died over there, just about two miles away from here.

998
00:57:23,396 --> 00:57:27,417
[SPEAKER_01]: My brother never married, and he was living with my married sister.

999
00:57:28,477 --> 00:57:31,618
[SPEAKER_02]: And then they couldn't handle him right, and then he went over.

1000
00:57:31,638 --> 00:57:32,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Did he play ball?

1001
00:57:33,553 --> 00:57:37,875
[SPEAKER_01]: But the funny part was, he was an underhanded pitcher, like Carl Mays.

1002
00:57:38,775 --> 00:57:40,776
[SPEAKER_01]: It was just local stuff there, you know.

1003
00:57:40,976 --> 00:57:42,037
[SPEAKER_01]: He never went out.

1004
00:57:42,837 --> 00:57:48,840
[SPEAKER_01]: But for the Church League or something like that, he was kind of unusual, because he'd come up like that, and go way down there.

1005
00:57:48,880 --> 00:57:50,601
[SPEAKER_01]: And nobody else done it around there.

1006
00:57:51,601 --> 00:57:58,925
[SPEAKER_03]: You were with the Reds in the season after the Black Sox World Series that year.

1007
00:57:59,405 --> 00:58:01,286
[SPEAKER_03]: Was there any talk of any kind of a

1008
00:58:02,329 --> 00:58:03,850
[SPEAKER_01]: throwing the series then?

1009
00:58:03,950 --> 00:58:14,958
[SPEAKER_01]: I never heard any particular because, like I say, I was there, I thought I was there a little over half a year, but I never heard anything particular.

1010
00:58:16,059 --> 00:58:24,905
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't say that I heard anything, any of the Reds accused them of anything.

1011
00:58:27,238 --> 00:58:37,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Offhanded, I heard, not from them, but from around ball players and that, that they claimed some of the guys weren't trying so hard.

1012
00:58:37,882 --> 00:58:38,702
[SPEAKER_01]: I heard that.

1013
00:58:39,682 --> 00:58:40,382
[SPEAKER_03]: The White Sox?

1014
00:58:40,442 --> 00:58:42,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1015
00:58:44,044 --> 00:58:47,965
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess that was Grisberg and some of those guys that were really accused of it.

1016
00:58:49,666 --> 00:58:51,286
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I didn't hear any more about that.

1017
00:58:51,506 --> 00:58:52,406
[SPEAKER_03]: How about Hal Chase?

1018
00:58:52,466 --> 00:58:53,407
[SPEAKER_03]: Were you with him at all?

1019
00:58:53,627 --> 00:58:53,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1020
00:58:54,627 --> 00:58:56,008
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a swell guy.

1021
00:58:56,128 --> 00:58:56,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that right?

1022
00:58:57,355 --> 00:58:58,855
[SPEAKER_03]: What about the charges against him?

1023
00:58:59,496 --> 00:59:00,336
[SPEAKER_01]: That I don't know.

1024
00:59:00,416 --> 00:59:02,336
[SPEAKER_01]: See, we had him after that.

1025
00:59:02,416 --> 00:59:08,418
[SPEAKER_01]: He had those at the White Sox, and he was with the Giants when I was there.

1026
00:59:08,438 --> 00:59:13,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Real nice guy, huh?

1027
00:59:13,899 --> 00:59:15,299
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course I know.

1028
00:59:15,840 --> 00:59:18,240
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought he was nice to me.

1029
00:59:19,080 --> 00:59:21,201
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't think he had any trouble with anybody.

1030
00:59:21,701 --> 00:59:24,842
[SPEAKER_03]: I've heard that from another old ball player, too, who was with Chase.

1031
00:59:26,191 --> 00:59:30,092
[SPEAKER_03]: Thought he was great and knew, couldn't say anything about these charges against him.

1032
00:59:30,132 --> 00:59:31,532
[SPEAKER_03]: I had no knowledge of that.

1033
00:59:31,552 --> 00:59:37,274
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't know a thing about his charges, but like I say, he got along with everybody fine.

1034
00:59:37,314 --> 00:59:39,214
[SPEAKER_01]: He seemed like he was a nice guy.

1035
00:59:40,454 --> 00:59:47,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever else he'd done or anything, why, they'd have to prove it to me because he didn't appear that way to me.

1036
00:59:48,976 --> 00:59:52,057
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah.

1037
00:59:53,113 --> 01:00:01,518
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm kind of glad to be talking about somebody that happened 50, 60, 70 years ago, you know, around here, where you don't get that anymore.

1038
01:00:02,259 --> 01:00:03,779
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm fascinated by it myself.

1039
01:00:03,860 --> 01:00:04,780
[SPEAKER_03]: I just hear you go on and on.

1040
01:00:04,800 --> 01:00:06,021
[SPEAKER_01]: How old are you, Mr. Murdock?

1041
01:00:06,061 --> 01:00:07,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you mind?

1042
01:00:08,782 --> 01:00:10,524
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll be 53 next month.

1043
01:00:10,884 --> 01:00:12,465
[SPEAKER_01]: And you go back that far.

1044
01:00:13,826 --> 01:00:15,066
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I mean, I've studied.

1045
01:00:15,186 --> 01:00:17,248
[SPEAKER_03]: The first ballgame I saw was 1929.

1046
01:00:18,388 --> 01:00:19,589
[SPEAKER_03]: And HODAP was in that, too.

1047
01:00:19,709 --> 01:00:20,710
[SPEAKER_03]: It was in Cleveland.

1048
01:00:20,910 --> 01:00:21,690
[SPEAKER_03]: I grew up in Cleveland.

1049
01:00:21,710 --> 01:00:22,691
[SPEAKER_03]: I was out in Link Park.

1050
01:00:23,345 --> 01:00:25,326
[SPEAKER_03]: Saw the Yankees play Cleveland.

1051
01:00:25,346 --> 01:00:28,888
[SPEAKER_01]: You told me you knew something about Crosley Field down there.

1052
01:00:29,108 --> 01:00:31,470
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I just was there once or twice.

1053
01:00:31,530 --> 01:00:33,711
[SPEAKER_03]: I came over here to see games.

1054
01:00:33,931 --> 01:00:35,052
[SPEAKER_03]: But I don't know.

1055
01:00:36,073 --> 01:00:36,753
[SPEAKER_03]: I was an American.

1056
01:00:36,773 --> 01:00:38,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Was it a big ride from Marietta down there?

1057
01:00:38,814 --> 01:00:41,176
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, about six hours.

1058
01:00:41,216 --> 01:00:42,336
[SPEAKER_03]: On all interstates.

1059
01:00:43,297 --> 01:00:44,758
[SPEAKER_03]: What are you going to do tonight?

1060
01:00:44,998 --> 01:00:45,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Stay at home?

1061
01:00:45,398 --> 01:00:47,059
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, we're staying up at a motel.

1062
01:00:47,539 --> 01:00:51,902
[SPEAKER_03]: And we're going to see Larry Kopf in the morning and go to see Jess Haines in the afternoon.

1063
01:00:53,835 --> 01:00:55,136
[SPEAKER_03]: He's my first Hall of Famer.

1064
01:00:56,097 --> 01:00:58,059
[SPEAKER_03]: Although I'm not sure he should be in the Hall of Fame.

1065
01:00:58,879 --> 01:01:00,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Will you say hello to Larry for me?

1066
01:01:00,881 --> 01:01:05,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Tell him to say Ed Ziggins said one of these days he's going to come out and see you anyway.

1067
01:01:05,586 --> 01:01:07,568
[SPEAKER_01]: He said he tried one time and he got lost.

1068
01:01:08,889 --> 01:01:11,071
[SPEAKER_01]: See my daughter lives out there.

1069
01:01:11,291 --> 01:01:12,032
[SPEAKER_01]: In Washington.

1070
01:01:14,048 --> 01:01:19,952
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, now you go down to the street here, and you go down there, and you turn, and I turn, and all of a sudden I'm out in the country someplace.

1071
01:01:20,052 --> 01:01:21,853
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said, I know he ain't down this far.

1072
01:01:21,893 --> 01:01:26,156
[SPEAKER_01]: So I come back, and I called him, and I said, I don't have time now, Larry.

1073
01:01:26,236 --> 01:01:28,078
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, but one of these days I'm going to see you.

1074
01:01:28,598 --> 01:01:29,919
[SPEAKER_01]: And he talked real nice.

1075
01:01:30,059 --> 01:01:31,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Larry talked real nice.

1076
01:01:31,360 --> 01:01:34,382
[SPEAKER_03]: I'd like to get the two of you together, and then I'd really have a tape.

1077
01:01:34,422 --> 01:01:38,625
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you can think of things I can't think of to ask, you see.

1078
01:01:38,665 --> 01:01:39,646
[SPEAKER_03]: You've had the experiences.

1079
01:01:40,840 --> 01:01:42,681
[SPEAKER_03]: heard and read about, and so I have to...

1080
01:01:42,761 --> 01:01:48,762
[SPEAKER_01]: The only thing I would have with Larry was, you know, a happy year playing alongside of him a little bit like that.

1081
01:01:48,882 --> 01:01:51,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And of course, he was older than I was.

1082
01:01:52,003 --> 01:01:53,944
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess he's 80-something now.

1083
01:01:53,964 --> 01:01:54,364
[SPEAKER_03]: 83, I think.

1084
01:01:54,384 --> 01:01:54,644
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1085
01:01:55,684 --> 01:02:05,247
[SPEAKER_01]: So he was older, and I guess he was married by the time I was single, and the rest of our life was apart.

1086
01:02:05,387 --> 01:02:08,148
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, we didn't have the same impact that way.

1087
01:02:09,213 --> 01:02:10,974
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, but Heine Groh, was he with you then?

1088
01:02:10,994 --> 01:02:12,135
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1089
01:02:12,195 --> 01:02:12,955
[SPEAKER_03]: What was Heine like?

1090
01:02:14,436 --> 01:02:15,397
[SPEAKER_03]: With that bottled bat?

1091
01:02:16,457 --> 01:02:17,538
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't have any trouble with Heine.

1092
01:02:17,558 --> 01:02:24,202
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, when he was sick and laying there and we talked quite a bit, he got along all right.

1093
01:02:25,663 --> 01:02:27,744
[SPEAKER_01]: I never had any trouble with anybody.

1094
01:02:27,784 --> 01:02:32,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Like I said, Pat Duncan, he got me bad, but he said I'd give him a dime.

1095
01:02:32,887 --> 01:02:33,928
[SPEAKER_03]: But all these Germans.

1096
01:02:34,567 --> 01:02:34,607
[SPEAKER_03]: Q.

1097
01:02:34,627 --> 01:02:40,128
[SPEAKER_03]: Were there a lot of Germans playing ball then for Cincinnati, or Kopf and Groh?

1098
01:02:40,268 --> 01:02:40,368
[SPEAKER_03]: A.

1099
01:02:41,768 --> 01:02:44,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, this is a German neighborhood to start with.

1100
01:02:44,749 --> 01:02:46,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Cincinnati was always German, you know.

1101
01:02:47,510 --> 01:02:49,910
[SPEAKER_01]: But, I don't know, particular.

1102
01:02:50,670 --> 01:02:50,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Q.

1103
01:02:50,830 --> 01:02:52,431
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a coincidence, probably, though.

1104
01:02:52,451 --> 01:02:53,051
[SPEAKER_03]: A. Yeah.

1105
01:02:53,491 --> 01:02:53,611
[SPEAKER_01]: Q.

1106
01:02:53,651 --> 01:02:55,631
[SPEAKER_00]: If you smoke, you go ahead and smoke.

1107
01:02:56,391 --> 01:02:57,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Have you smoked?

1108
01:02:59,252 --> 01:03:00,692
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm supposed to be cutting down.

1109
01:03:00,932 --> 01:03:02,193
[SPEAKER_00]: My husband doesn't approve.

1110
01:03:02,213 --> 01:03:03,453
[SPEAKER_01]: But I will.

1111
01:03:04,607 --> 01:03:05,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Toughest pitcher?

1112
01:03:05,487 --> 01:03:08,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Toughest pitcher I think that I ever had against was Phil Douglas.

1113
01:03:09,289 --> 01:03:09,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Shuffling Phil.

1114
01:03:09,969 --> 01:03:12,270
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a spitball pitcher that threw sidearm

1115
01:03:25,494 --> 01:03:27,875
[SPEAKER_01]: And he wasn't afraid to touch you up once in a while.

1116
01:03:27,955 --> 01:03:33,137
[SPEAKER_01]: So that spitter come at your head and would go over the outside corner of the plane.

1117
01:03:33,497 --> 01:03:35,798
[SPEAKER_01]: If it didn't break, you'd have to sit down.

1118
01:03:36,058 --> 01:03:37,698
[SPEAKER_01]: So I thought he was kind of tough.

1119
01:03:38,039 --> 01:03:39,419
[SPEAKER_03]: That the spitter went down?

1120
01:03:39,939 --> 01:03:42,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, whatever way he threw it, see.

1121
01:03:42,540 --> 01:03:45,001
[SPEAKER_01]: If he threw it sidearm, it would go out.

1122
01:03:45,061 --> 01:03:46,442
[SPEAKER_01]: If he threw it overhand, it would go down.

1123
01:03:46,462 --> 01:03:52,744
[SPEAKER_01]: But if he threw it half sidearm and half down, he'd turn it around your head and it would go over the outside corner.

1124
01:03:54,023 --> 01:03:55,484
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought it was very tough for me.

1125
01:03:55,745 --> 01:03:56,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, all of them are tough.

1126
01:03:57,046 --> 01:03:58,287
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't hit them as good.

1127
01:03:58,647 --> 01:03:59,428
[SPEAKER_01]: What happened in 1928?

1128
01:03:59,508 --> 01:04:00,509
[SPEAKER_01]: We had that great year at Louisville.

